<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gamla Uppsala</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Old Uppsala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gamla <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> is a village and ancient royal site north of modern Uppsala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Its great burial mounds date from the 5th and 6th centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> described a pagan temple there in the 11th century. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a> kings and from 1164 of Sweden's first archbishopric, moved to the present Uppsala in the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamla-uppsala.jpg" alt="Gamla Uppsala" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: OlofE · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gamla_uppsala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
